{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 877 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "accb4198b91642bb1ec7792ab809180ed6ad452c9eca908561a210b592e74b6f" :edb/db-before-t 876 :edb/db-after-t 877 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534189 50 #inst "2026-10-06T09:09:02.585Z" 13194139534189 true] [17592186059600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281318 13194139534189 true] [17592186068619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281342 13194139534189 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281301 13194139534189 true] [17592186083493 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534189 true] [17592186083493 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nEvaluate $(\\frac{10}{6})$." 13194139534189 false] [17592186083493 1100 "Evaluate $(\\frac{10}{6}).$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186083493 1145 "First, note that $(\\frac{10}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{10}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{4!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{4!\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\,040}{24} \\\\  & =210.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186087783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281335 13194139534189 true] [17592186096553 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281321 13194139534189 true] [17592186096760 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281315 13194139534189 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281328 13194139534189 true] [17592186115139 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281308 13194139534189 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281294 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281295 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1089 "q-108605" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1113 #uuid "08681bdb-d18d-52f4-853d-7f023cb7b260" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281294 1145 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281296 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281297 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281298 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281299 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281300 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281300 13194139534189 true] [17592186281295 1110 #uuid "5ea811c7-e487-5f42-b9f8-005e922a4e22" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281296 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281296 1111 #uuid "72c79874-f9f1-571a-b9a5-16ca24be562c" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281297 1034 "I, III, and IV only" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281297 1111 #uuid "e2611dd2-9bd3-5830-b4c8-82edf7164be5" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281298 1034 "III only" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281298 1111 #uuid "a1aa88e2-823e-5c97-a817-7ba17055a1a3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281299 1034 "None" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281299 1111 #uuid "0d7b628b-0379-50f2-bc2c-3ee818867d54" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281300 1034 "II only" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281300 1111 #uuid "1e2bb27c-11ae-5e64-b9c0-3f801903a126" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281302 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1089 "q-27448" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/f5b33723509f58055f0ded0c575ea097cc90ff05fcfc72fc3270553248de0f2b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1113 #uuid "3bdef897-6fb1-52b2-84e8-2991640d04c4" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281301 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281303 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281304 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281305 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281306 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281307 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281305 13194139534189 true] [17592186281302 1110 #uuid "1c5edda9-092a-5e25-8536-488b61b9231a" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281303 1034 "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281303 1111 #uuid "21df5827-52f6-57ec-9c9e-aa93d7e9216e" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281304 1034 "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}-2" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281304 1111 #uuid "f114e9b8-cac1-50ba-a395-e381d8bb6db2" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281305 1034 "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}-3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281305 1111 #uuid "553a781b-c50b-598b-ba5a-08fee0b0b841" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281306 1034 "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281306 1111 #uuid "6dd0672a-5dce-5bd5-929c-bf39bffcd9d7" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281307 1034 "y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+2" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281307 1111 #uuid "92f3f9e1-30c6-551d-9862-f9ff94cab461" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281309 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1089 "q-3304" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1100 "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{x}\\cdot {4}^{2x-1}=32.$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1113 #uuid "bc33708c-6675-5c4d-add3-ff1acd566a68" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281308 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $8={2}^{3}$, $4={2}^{2}$ and $32={2}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {({2}^{2})}^{2x-1}={2}^{5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the product rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ {2}^{3x+2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2(2x-1) & =5 \\\\ 3x+4x-2 & =5 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281310 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281311 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281312 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281313 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281314 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281312 13194139534189 true] [17592186281309 1110 #uuid "3f3158eb-fd35-5f38-b15a-caca8853faee" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281310 1034 "3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281310 1111 #uuid "bdf1b5dd-b580-56be-8e44-66f1fbd18490" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281311 1034 "2" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281311 1111 #uuid "b43d2518-4b7b-5398-9ac9-b3ed00aa6344" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281312 1034 "1" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281312 1111 #uuid "29e0dae8-cafd-536d-bef0-cee23d8df621" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281313 1034 "-3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281313 1111 #uuid "cd6d04a8-16c6-5456-bd4f-189120503388" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281314 1034 "5" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281314 1111 #uuid "3af06527-486e-5c9d-95de-5c920ccccf54" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281316 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1089 "q-340690" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1100 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1113 #uuid "1828f73d-8e30-58d0-95fc-8bce2cfc9181" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281315 1145 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281317 13194139534189 true] [17592186281316 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534189 true] [17592186281316 1041 "field-1" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281316 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281317 13194139534189 true] [17592186281316 1110 #uuid "0b829935-3485-5a9c-8ecb-106aa4a44b89" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281317 1034 "-28(1-7x)^3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281317 1111 #uuid "b656a3a4-5efd-573f-9a31-752b8af7e196" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281319 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1089 "q-341825" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1100 "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}$ when $x=0.$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1113 #uuid "958c8a96-376c-5bbd-bca4-7ca1bae02381" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281318 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(0)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $1.$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281320 13194139534189 true] [17592186281319 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534189 true] [17592186281319 1041 "field-1" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281319 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281320 13194139534189 true] [17592186281319 1110 #uuid "1b947dfa-2181-55d4-a6cc-1928297d422e" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281320 1034 "1" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281320 1111 #uuid "806dd40a-45da-5a8a-a040-ef00812b03e8" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281322 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1089 "q-49235" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1100 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1113 #uuid "00fd6781-496f-504f-8d8f-9433de9018a5" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281321 1145 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281323 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281324 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281325 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281326 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281327 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281324 13194139534189 true] [17592186281322 1110 #uuid "ddf17f9d-4e6e-5a31-ad2d-5723949d666e" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281323 1034 "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b=e+1$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281323 1111 #uuid "ea2b7d0b-3612-5bee-8ccd-a56606c8af65" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281324 1034 "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281324 1111 #uuid "c28b45f8-8324-5eb1-9c9c-334f4006b896" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281325 1034 "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b={e}^{2}$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281325 1111 #uuid "89f95166-9a92-5dfe-b19d-17dd972877b9" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281326 1034 "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b={e}^{2}$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281326 1111 #uuid "f6be9a2b-cbbe-5b8f-afec-ff1fc8fcbd0e" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281327 1034 "$f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+e)$ and $b=2e$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281327 1111 #uuid "ee50c769-188e-5968-b82d-3d9c5dc8abf7" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281329 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1089 "q-64730" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1113 #uuid "ef571d7e-15de-5505-9d00-d15c6ffa6363" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281328 1145 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281330 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281331 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281332 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281333 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281334 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281331 13194139534189 true] [17592186281329 1110 #uuid "18357b7a-266a-57cd-94ca-5cb250dacb07" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281330 1034 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281330 1111 #uuid "e50b1afc-26db-5300-95e4-d136af180bb7" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281331 1034 "1" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281331 1111 #uuid "4be21ce8-b07d-501b-b492-b7775e7ff8e9" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281332 1034 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281332 1111 #uuid "f7676bcf-48a9-5fe9-b2ad-9877140590d8" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281333 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281333 1111 #uuid "111f812b-1ee8-5c4b-8c26-5c7ce9b79659" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281334 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281334 1111 #uuid "024bca2e-a111-5a14-b298-87c1348bde31" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281336 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1089 "q-6912" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1100 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3{x}^{2}+1}.$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1113 #uuid "32ba2264-1073-53c2-9413-bebb41a92212" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281335 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281337 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281338 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281339 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281340 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281341 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1041 "selection" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281341 13194139534189 true] [17592186281336 1110 #uuid "4e3d6524-f45c-5166-a47a-2e3b19a6d6f0" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281337 1034 "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281337 1111 #uuid "39e6c3a2-e3a1-53c6-814d-7982a5f05597" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281338 1034 "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281338 1111 #uuid "f25f276a-ff70-5532-b497-89768d96d295" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281339 1034 "x=-3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281339 1111 #uuid "fd9ec87d-a2fb-5f41-b5c1-7d480630bbec" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281340 1034 "x=-3,\\,x=3" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534189 true] [17592186281340 1111 #uuid "9be9616c-3a2f-58b8-b272-43e6fa6f4846" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281341 1034 "The function has no vertical asymptotes" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534189 true] [17592186281341 1111 #uuid "3a96f723-d9fe-5114-8287-a025721d0042" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281343 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1089 "q-78131" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980882/assets/3c0dc4b8f7f45a5bc1177778431eccf9bcaac1bf6d520d605f9e6bc08d5b1d91.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1113 #uuid "07cdba0d-b698-52a7-a7d7-7d131e11e8f5" 13194139534189 true] [17592186281342 1145 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534189 true] [17592186281343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281344 13194139534189 true] [17592186281343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281345 13194139534189 true] [17592186281343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281346 13194139534189 true] [17592186281343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281347 13194139534189 true] [17592186281343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186